# The American Bahá'í, Volume 57, Number 5, October/November/December 2026
# Feature articles transcribed from the issue. Blocks: p paragraph, h subhead, q pull quote, qa question, a answer (name, text), note.

ISSUE = dict(
    title="The American Bahá'í",
    theme="The Arts Issue",
    vol="Volume 57, Number 5",
    date="October / November / December 2026",
    url="www.bahai.us/stories",
    about="This issue explores the many ways creative expression can contribute to the transformation of individuals and communities, from music and visual art to the creative practices woven into grassroots efforts. The stories that follow offer glimpses of communities drawing on imagination, art and spiritual insights to contribute to the betterment of humanity.",
    cover_artist="Juliet Bogan",
    cover_note="As an Iranian-American Bahá'í artist, much of my painting practice attempts to imagine a homeland I do not know, while reaching for a future uniting people, land and spirit. This painting is simultaneously nostalgic and hopeful, evoking a community built into the landscape. Natural beauty merges with the beauty of service and stewardship of the earth, inspired by community-building activities we are undertaking in New York City. References range from my landscape photography in the Northeastern United States, to photographs sourced of Iran and Yemen where my grandparents grew up, and finally to the curving calligraphy of the Greatest Name, which I used as a template for the tumbling hills of the farmland.",
    epigraph="Indeed the arts as a whole, so integral a part of the development of a community from the start, stand out in such settings as an important means of generating joy, strengthening bonds of unity, disseminating knowledge, and consolidating understanding…",
    epigraph_src="The Universal House of Justice",
    publisher="Published by the National Spiritual Assembly of the Bahá'ís of the United States, 1233 Central St., Evanston, IL 60201. Entire contents copyright © 2026 by the National Spiritual Assembly of the Bahá'ís of the United States. World rights reserved.",
)

# Short passages from "Abiding in God's Law: Artistic expressions of the human spirit", pp. 32–33
VOICES = [
    ("All Art is a gift of the Holy Spirit. When this light shines through the mind of a musician, it manifests itself in beautiful harmonies. Again, shining through the mind of a poet, it is seen in fine poetry and poetic prose. When the Light of the Sun of Truth inspires the mind of a painter, he produces marvellous pictures. These gifts are fulfilling their highest purpose, when showing forth the praise of God.",
     "'Abdu'l-Bahá, in the compilation The Importance of the Arts in Promoting the Faith"),
    ("I rejoice to hear that thou takest pains with thine art, for in this wonderful new age, art is worship. The more thou strivest to perfect it, the closer wilt thou come to God.",
     "'Abdu'l-Bahá, in the compilation The Importance of the Arts in Promoting the Faith"),
    ("The source of crafts, sciences and arts is the power of reflection. Make ye every effort that out of this ideal mine there may gleam forth such pearls of wisdom and utterance as will promote the well-being and harmony of all the kindreds of the earth.",
     "Bahá'u'lláh, Bahá'í Sacred Writings, Attaining Social Progress and Well-Being: Excerpts from Words of Paradise"),
    ("The friends are also asked to give greater attention to the use of the arts, not only for proclamation, but also for the work in expansion and consolidation. The graphic and performing arts and literature have played, and can play, a major role in extending the influence of the Cause. At the level of folk art, this possibility can be pursued in every part of the world, whether it be in villages, towns or cities.",
     "The Universal House of Justice, April 21, 1996, to the Bahá'ís of the World"),
    ("Mortal charm shall fade away, roses shall give way to thorns, and beauty and youth shall live their day and be no more. But that which eternally endureth is the Beauty of the True One, for its splendor perisheth not and its glory lasteth forever; its charm is all-powerful and its attraction infinite.",
     "'Abdu'l-Bahá, Selections from the Writings of 'Abdu'l-Bahá, no. 175"),
    ("Music is an important means to the education and development of humanity, but the only true way is through the Teachings of God. Music is like this glass, which is perfectly pure and polished. It is precisely like this pure chalice before us, and the Teachings of God, the utterances of God, are like the water.",
     "'Abdu'l-Bahá, in the compilation Music"),
]

GREGORY_EVENTS = [
    (1874,"Born in Charleston, South Carolina","Born to a family that valued education, he went on to Avery Institute in Charleston, Fisk University and Howard University Law School. In his childhood a white mob lynched his grandfather and spared his grandmother."),
    (1879,"His father, Ebenezer George, dies","About 1879. Louis was about five."),
    (1881,"His mother marries George Gregory","On July 14. Louis later took his stepfather's surname."),
    (1891,"His mother dies; he graduates from Avery","His mother, Mary Elizabeth, died of spinal meningitis. The same year he graduated from Avery, then the leading school for Black students in Charleston, and entered Fisk."),
    (1896,"Graduates from Fisk University","In June. His stepfather paid for his first year; he paid for the rest with scholarships and by tailoring for other students, a trade his stepfather had apprenticed him to. Afterward he taught at Avery."),
    (1902,"Law degree from Howard University","He received his law degree in the spring and was admitted to practice that October. He opened a Washington law office with James A. Cobb."),
    (1906,"Vice president of the Howard law alumni association","The same year he joined the U.S. Treasury Department."),
    (1907,"Hears Lua Getsinger speak on the Bahá'í Faith","The same year he was elected vice president of the Bethel Literary and Historical Society."),
    (1909,"Becomes a Bahá'í in Washington, D.C.","He began teaching the Bahá'í Faith at once within his professional, academic and social circles. As President of the Bethel Literary and Historical Society he arranged four landmark presentations. In November a Tablet from ‘Abdu'l-Bahá urged him to work for unity between Black and white."),
    (1910,"First independent travel-teaching journey","He shaped his talks and newspaper articles to each audience, on racial amity and unity, comparative religion and the Bahá'í principles. “In every city, people were found who accepted the great Message.” That March, Washington held its first deliberately interracial Bahá'í meeting. In November he spoke at Morris Brown AME Church in Charleston and met Alonzo Twine, the first known Bahá'í in South Carolina."),
    (1911,"Pilgrimage; meets ‘Abdu'l-Bahá","He sailed from New York on March 25, arrived in Ramleh, Egypt, on April 10 and met ‘Abdu'l-Bahá. His account, A Heavenly Vista: The Pilgrimage of Louis G. Gregory, was published in Washington the same year. That February he had been elected to fill a vacancy on Washington's Working Committee."),
    (1912,"Marries Louise Mathew; arranges ‘Abdu'l-Bahá's talk at Howard University for around 1,600","In April he was elected to the Executive Board of Bahá'í Temple Unity, the national body that preceded the National Spiritual Assembly. On April 23, at a Persian Legation luncheon in Washington, ‘Abdu'l-Bahá seated him at the table. The marriage, on September 27 and encouraged by ‘Abdu'l-Bahá, was the first interracial marriage of Black and white Bahá'ís."),
    (1913,"Teaches in Cleveland, Ohio","Among those he taught were the parents of Lydia Martin, who would bring him to Pine Bluff, Arkansas, twenty-six years later."),
    (1916,"First Tablets of the Divine Plan arrive","‘Abdu'l-Bahá's first Tablets of the Divine Plan reached America, among them one to the believers of the Southern States, sent through Joseph Hannen. By the end of the year Gregory had travelled through 14 of the 16 Southern states."),
    (1917,"A second round of travel through the South","In January he wrote to President Wilson about conditions in the South."),
    (1919,"Nine months teaching across the South with Roy Williams","Begun in March, the tour became part of the program for teaching in the South that followed the unveiling of the Tablets of the Divine Plan at the national convention that April. The Star of the West reported that the two “are sending in lists of those interested, showing splendid results, and are most happy in the work.” On April 27 he gave “The Power of the Holy Spirit” at the Bahá'í Congress in New York."),
    (1920,"‘Abdu'l-Bahá: “a heart like unto transparent water”","“He is like unto pure gold. That is why he is acceptable in any market and is current in every country.”"),
    (1921,"First Race Amity Convention, Washington, D.C.","Held May 19 to 21 at the First Congregational Church. Agnes Parsons convened it at ‘Abdu'l-Bahá's request and planned it with Gregory, who spoke and later reported the proceedings. In April he was appointed to the national teaching committee for the Southern states, and from that fall into 1922 he made a long tour of U.S. cities and Vancouver."),
    (1922,"First African American elected to the National Spiritual Assembly","He served three terms: 1922–24, 1927–32 and 1939–46."),
    (1924,"Shares platforms with Alain Locke","He toured the country speaking, and appeared with Alain Locke at the 1924 race amity conventions in New York and Philadelphia."),
    (1927,"Executive secretary, National Committee on Inter-racial Amity","He was appointed to the committee and became its executive secretary."),
    (1928,"“Inter-Racial Amity” in The Bahá'í World","His account of the race amity work appeared in Volume II. The same year he and Mariam Haney edited a compilation on race amity for the committee."),
    (1929,"His stepfather's funeral in Charleston","About 1,000 people attended the funeral of George Gregory."),
    (1931,"Eighteen-day teaching trip with Willard McKay","McKay had lost his job as a professor because of his outspoken race amity advocacy and cross-racial relationships. Described as “two tall men, one dark, one very fair, both radiant,” they collaborated closely and regularly, to the astonishment of those who met them. In December he helped organize a study group in Atlanta."),
    (1937,"Pioneers to Haiti with Louise, at 62 and 71","Louise, an experienced international pioneer, spoke fluent French. They held study classes twice a week for the young intelligentsia, and found a translator to hold classes in Creole. Refused permission to hold public meetings, they left on April 10, having arrived in January."),
    (1939,"Two months at the college in Pine Bluff, Arkansas","Lydia Martin, Dean of Women at the Arkansas Agricultural, Mechanical, and Normal College, arranged for him to speak at its Religious Emphasis Week. He stayed two months."),
    (1940,"Integrated meetings in Atlanta","He helped settle a dispute in Atlanta in favor of integrated meetings, and served on the Race Unity Committee with Dorothy Beecher Baker."),
    (1944,"Recording secretary of the National Spiritual Assembly","That winter he toured the Bahá'í communities of South Carolina."),
    (1948,"Suffers a stroke","In December."),
    (1951,"Passes away; named a Hand of the Cause of God","He died in Eliot, Maine, on July 30. Shoghi Effendi, Guardian of the Bahá'í Faith, appointed him a Hand of the Cause after his passing. In November a memorial service was held at the Bahá'í House of Worship in Wilmette."),
    (1956,"Louise Gregory dies in Eliot, Maine","On May 20."),
    (1968,"Louis G. Gregory Award for Service to Humanity established","First presented that December."),
    (1972,"Louis Gregory Bahá'í Institute dedicated in Hemingway","An education and retreat center a few hours north of Charleston, where his childhood home is now a Bahá'í museum."),
    (1982,"Biography: To Move the World","Gayle Morrison's To Move the World: Louis G. Gregory and the Advancement of Racial Unity in America."),
    (1984,"Radio Bahá'í WLGI begins broadcasting","A noncommercial station serving Northeastern South Carolina and Southeastern North Carolina, also streamed at radiobahai.us. Sacred writings are part of its daily programming."),
    (2003,"His boyhood home opens as a museum in Charleston","The Charleston Bahá'í community bought the house at auction in the late 1980s. The Louis G. Gregory Bahá'í Museum was dedicated in February 2003."),
    (2022,"Institute's 50th anniversary","Friends who had trained at the Institute decades earlier returned to celebrate, and to consider its future."),
    (2026,"Summer of Service hosts 25 youth","Youth of diverse races and faiths studied, prayed and “built friendships across what society deems as impermeable racial walls.”")]
